Electric field between the plates is

\begin{aligned}
& =$\frac{\sigma}{2 \varepsilon_0}-\frac{(-\sigma)}{2 \varepsilon_0}$ \\
& =$\frac{\sigma}{\varepsilon_0} \text{ volt }$ / $\text{ meter }$
\end{aligned}

<img src="https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/aiims/g_uov79EhuHOsX1OsF4HJ4QwWOXDw-hHWY0f3AYsW8A.original.fullsize.png"><br>

Since both the metal spheres are connected to the same conducting wire, both of them will be having same charge on them. Let the charge on each of them be $q.
Then, the $electric field intensities are given by,

\begin{aligned}
& E_a=$\frac{k q}{a^2}$, \quad E_b=$\frac{k q}{b^2}$ \\
& \therefore $\frac{E_a}{E_b}=\frac{b^2}{a^2}$
\end{aligned}
